      "content": "Avoiding the \"limitation\" disclosure entirely is risky because reviewers will inevitably spot the low $n$, and it can cross from omitting a flaw to inflating your claims unintentionally. Readers are unlikely to accept a hidden assumption that a large sample *should* have been used, which may undermine your own credibility. It is usually safer to briefly acknowledge the constraint as a purposeful choice for high internal validity rather than ignoring it completely, since honesty about strategic trade-offs often strengthens your argument.",
